== Finite generation of cohomology <sec:finite-generation>

In this section we present Evens' proof [@bib:Evens1961] that the cohomology
ring of a finite group over a Noetherian ring is finitely generated#idx(
  "cohomology",
  "finite generation of",
)#idx("finite", "generation of cohomology"). For Venkov's topological proof see
Section~@sec:equivariant-cohomology.

#theorem(title: [Evens])[
  If $G$ is a finite group and $M$ is an $R G$-module which is Noetherian as a
  module for $R$ then $H^* (G, M)$ is Noetherian as a module for $H^* (G, R)$.
] <th:evens-finite-generation>

#corollary[
  If $R$ is a Noetherian ring then $H^* (G, R)$ is finitely generated by
  homogeneous elements, as a ring over $R$.
] <cor:finitely-generated-ring>

#remark(kind: "Warning")[
  If $R$ is not Noetherian then $H^* (G, R)$ is not necessarily finitely
  generated over $R$. For example if $G = ZZ slash p$ and
  $R = ZZ plus.o (ZZ slash p)^infinity$ (with multiplication given by
  $(a, b) . (c, d) = (a c, c b + a d)$) then there are infinitely many new
  generators in degree one.
]

The corollary follows from the theorem (with $M = R$) by applying the following
lemma.

#lemma[
  If $H = plus.o.big_(n gt.eq 0) H^n$ is a graded ring and $H$ is Noetherian (as
  a ring), then $H^0$ is Noetherian and $H$ is finitely generated over $H^0$ by
  homogeneous elements.
] <lem:graded-noetherian>

#proof[
  #source(139)Since there is a surjective map $H ->> H^0$, $H^0$ is Noetherian.
  Let $H^+ = plus.o.big_(n > 0) H^n$. This is an ideal in $H$, and is hence
  finitely generated. Each generator is a finite sum of homogeneous elements,
  and so $H^+$ is generated as an ideal by homogeneous elements
  $a_1, dots, a_n$, say. Then $H$ is generated as a ring over $H^0$ by
  $1, a_1, dots, a_n$.
]

To prove the theorem, we first reduce to the Sylow $p$-subgroups of $G$, and
then we proceed by induction on the order of the $p$-group, using the spectral
sequence coming from a central element of order $p$. The crux of the proof is
the fact that this spectral sequence stops at some finite page, and this
involves the use of the Evens norm map.

#proof(head: smallcaps[Reduction to Sylow $p$-subgroups], closed: false)[
  By Proposition~3.7.10 of Volume~I, for any $alpha$ of positive degree in
  $H^* (G, M)$ we have $abs(G) . alpha = 0$. Thus if we set
  $ H^+ (G, M) = plus.o.big_(n > 0) H^n (G, M) $
  then we have
  $ H^+ (G, M) = plus.o.big_(p divides |G|) H^+ (G, M)_((p)) $
  where $H^+ (G, M)_((p))$ denotes the elements in $H^+ (G, M)$ annihilated by
  $|G|_p$. Again using Proposition~3.7.10 of Volume~I, we see that if $P$ is a
  Sylow $p$-subgroup of $G$ then
  $ res_(G, P) : H^* (G, M)_((p)) -> H^* (P, M) $
  is injective, and
  $ H^* (P, M) = H^* (G, M)_((p)) plus.o T(M) $
  where
  $ T(M) = Ker(tr_(P, G) : H^* (P, M) -> H^* (G, M)). $
  Thus we need to show that if $H^* (G, M)_((p)) plus.o T(M)$ is Noetherian as a
  module over $H^* (G, R)_((p)) plus.o T(R)$ then $H^* (G, M)_((p))$ is
  Noetherian as a module over $H^* (G, R)_((p))$.

  Now by Lemma~3.7.9~(ii) of Volume~I, we have
  $
    tr_(P, G) (beta) . alpha
    = tr_(P, G) (beta . res_(G, P) (alpha))
  $
  so that
  $ T(R) . H^+ (G, M)_((p)) subset.eq T(M). $
  We also have
  $ H^+ (G, R)_((p)) . H^+ (G, M)_((p)) subset.eq H^+ (G, M)_((p)). $
  #source(140)Thus if $A$ is an $H^+ (G, R)_((p))$-submodule of
  $H^+ (G, M)_((p))$ then
  $
    H^+ (P, R) . A & = (H^+ (G, R)_((p)) plus.o T(R)) . A \
                   & = H^+ (G, R)_((p)) . A plus.o T(R) . A
                     subset.eq H^+ (G, M)_((p)) plus.o T(M).
  $
  It follows that if $A$ is a proper submodule of $A'$ then $H^+ (P, R) . A$ is
  a proper submodule of $H^+ (P, R) . A'$, and so infinite ascending chains in
  $H^+ (G, M)_((p))$ give rise to infinite ascending chains in $H^+ (P, M)$.
]

#proof(head: smallcaps[The case $G = P$ is a $p$-group])[
  We proceed by induction on the order of $P$. If $P = 1$ the theorem is
  trivial. If $P > 1$, then let $Z$ be a cyclic subgroup of order $p$ in $Z(P)$,
  and examine the spectral sequence of the central extension
  $1 -> Z -> P -> P slash Z -> 1$. Set
  $
    E_2 (R) = H^* (P slash Z, H^* (Z, R)), quad
    E_2 (M) = H^* (P slash Z, H^* (Z, M)).
  $
  Then $E_2 (R)$ has a ring structure over which $E_2 (M)$ is a module (see
  Section~@sec:spectral-products).

  Now recall that the vertical edge homomorphism is just the restriction map, so
  that the following diagram commutes:
  $ #edge-restriction $
  Let $sigma in H^2 (Z, R)$ be the element described in Corollary~3.5.4 of
  Volume~I, with the property that cup product with $sigma$ induces an
  isomorphism
  $ H^r (Z, -) -> H^(r+2) (Z, -) $
  for all $r$ and for all coefficients. Then setting $n = |P : Z|$, we have
  $ sigma^n = res_(P, Z) norm_(Z, P) (sigma) $
  so that the element $xi = sigma^n$ is an element of degree $2n$ in
  $Im(res_(P, Z))$, i.e., in $E_infinity^(0, 2n) (R)$.

  Set
  $ E'_2 (M) = plus.o.big_(0 lt.eq q lt.eq 2n - 1) E_2^(* q) (M) $
  so that
  $ E_2 (M) = plus.o.big_(N gt.eq 0) xi^N . E'_2 (M). $

  Since each $H^q (Z, M)$ is a subquotient of $M$ (Corollary~3.5.2 of Volume~I),
  it is Noetherian over $R$, and so by the inductive hypothesis each
  $E_2^(* q) (M) = H^* (P slash Z, H^q (Z, M))$ is Noetherian over
  $E_2^(* 0) (R) = H^* (P slash Z, R)$. Thus $E'_2 (M)$ is Noetherian over
  $E_2^(* 0) (R)$. It now follows from the Hilbert basis theorem that $E_2 (M)$
  is Noetherian over $E_2^(* 0) (R)[xi]$.

  #source(141)Now recall that
  $
    E_1 (M) = Z_1 (M) supset.eq Z_2 (M) supset.eq dots.c supset.eq B_2 (M)
    supset.eq B_1 (M) = 0
  $
  with $E_2 (M) = Z_2 (M) slash B_2 (M)$. The product rule
  (Theorem~@th:spectral-products) shows that each $Z_r (M)$ and each $B_r (M)$
  is a submodule of $E_2 (M)$ as an $E_2^(* 0) (R)[xi]$-module (note that if
  each $Z_r (M)$ and $B_r (M)$ were an $E_2 (R)$-module the proof would be
  easier to word, but it seems that there is no reason why this should be the
  case). Since $E_infinity (M) = Z_infinity (M) slash B_infinity (M)$ is a
  subquotient of $E_2 (M)$, it is a Noetherian $E_2^(* 0) (R)[xi]$-module. Now
  there is a surjective ring homomorphism
  $ E_2^(* 0) (R)[xi] -> E_infinity^(* 0) (R)[xi] $
  whose kernel acts trivially on $E_infinity (M)$. Thus $E_infinity (M)$ is
  Noetherian as an $E_infinity^(* 0) (R)[xi]$-module, and hence Noetherian as an
  $E_infinity (R)$-module.

  Now $H^* (P, M)$ has a filtration whose filtered quotients are
  $
    E_infinity^(p q) (M) tilde.equiv F^p H^(p+q) (P, M) slash
    F^(p+1) H^(p+q) (P, M),
  $
  so we need to "unfilter" this statement. Suppose
  $A_1 subset A_2 subset dots.c subset H^* (P, M)$ is an infinite ascending
  chain of submodules for the action of $H^* (P, R)$. Let
  $F^p A_i = A_i inter F^p H^* (P, M)$ and
  $
    B_i = plus.o.big_(p gt.eq 0) F^p A_i slash F^(p+1) A_i
    subset.eq E_infinity (M).
  $
  If $x$ is an element of $A_(i+1)$ not in $A_i$, then for some $p$,
  $x in F^p A_(i+1)$ but $x in.not F^(p+1) A_(i+1) + F^p A_i$ and so
  $x + F^(p+1) A_(i+1)$ is not in the image of the inclusion
  $
    F^p A_i slash F^(p+1) A_i arrow.r.hook
    F^p A_(i+1) slash F^(p+1) A_(i+1).
  $
  Thus $B_(i+1)$ properly contains $B_i$ and so we have an infinite ascending
  chain of $E_infinity (R)$-submodules of $E_infinity (M)$. We know that this is
  impossible, and so this completes the proof of
  Theorem~@th:evens-finite-generation.
]

#corollary[
  If $G$ is finite and $M$ and $N$ are $R G$-modules such that $Hom_R (M, N)$ is
  a Noetherian $R$-module, then $Ext^*_(R G) (M, N)$ is Noetherian as a module
  over the ring $H^* (G, R)$ via cup product, and also as a module over the ring
  $Ext^*_(R G) (M, M)$ by Yoneda composition. If $Hom_R (M, M)$ is a Noetherian
  $R$-module then the ring $Ext^*_(R G) (M, M)$ is Noetherian as a module over
  its centre $Z Ext^*_(R G) (M, M)$.
] <cor:ext-noetherian>

#proof[
  We have
  $ H^* (G, Hom_R (M, N)) tilde.equiv Ext^*_(R G) (M, N) $
  (see Proposition~3.1.8 of Volume~I), so that by
  Theorem~@th:evens-finite-generation, $Ext^*_(R G) (M, N)$ is Noetherian as a
  module over $H^* (G, R)$. According to Section~3.2 of Volume~I, the cup
  product action of $H^* (G, R)$ on $Ext^*_(R G) (M, N)$ factors as the map
  $
    H^* (G, R) = Ext^*_(R G) (R, R) limits(arrow.r.long)^(times.o M)
    Ext^*_(R G) (M, M)
  $
  followed by Yoneda composition. Therefore $Ext^*_(R G) (M, N)$ is Noetherian
  as a module over $Ext^*_(R G) (M, M)$. Finally, the image of even degree
  elements under #source(142)the above map lie in the centre of
  $Ext^*_(R G) (M, M)$ by Section~3.2 of Volume~I, and so $Ext^*_(R G) (M, M)$
  is Noetherian as a module over its centre.
]

#corollary[
  If $H$ is a subgroup of a finite group $G$ and $R$ is a Noetherian ring, then
  $H^* (H, R)$ is finitely generated as a module over $H^* (G, R)$ (via the
  restriction map).
] <cor:subgroup-finitely-generated>

#proof[
  The Eckmann–Shapiro isomorphism (see Corollary~3.3.2 of Volume~I)
  $ H^* (H, R) tilde.equiv H^* (G, R_H up^G) $
  is an isomorphism of modules over $H^* (G, R)$. Since $R_H up^G$ is a
  Noetherian $R$-module, the result follows directly from
  Theorem~@th:evens-finite-generation.
]

Note that Evens' Theorem applies just as well to hypercohomology (see
Section~2.7 of Volume~I) of chain complexes of $R G$-modules. In this context, a
chain complex of $R G$-modules is thought of as Noetherian as an $R$-module if
it is non-zero in only finitely many degrees, and each non-zero module is
Noetherian.

#theorem[
  Suppose that $bold(C)$ and $bold(D)$ are chain complexes of $R G$-modules. If
  the complex $bold(Hom)_R (bold(C), bold(D))$ is a Noetherian $R$-module (and
  in particular non-zero in only finitely many degrees) then
  $Ext^*_(R G) (bold(C), bold(D))$ is Noetherian as a module over $H^* (G, R)$
  via cup product, and also as a module over $Ext^*_(R G) (bold(C), bold(C))$ by
  Yoneda composition.
] <th:hypercohomology-noetherian>

#proof[
  The proof is exactly the same as the proof in the module case. Namely, the
  Lyndon–Hochschild–Serre spectral sequence works just as well for
  hypercohomology as for cohomology, and we have
  $
    H^* (G, bold(Hom)_R (bold(C), bold(D)))
    tilde.equiv Ext^*_(R G) (bold(C), bold(D)).
  $
]

#corollary[
  Suppose that $R$ is a Noetherian ring with the property that for each prime
  $p$ dividing $|G|$, either $p$ is invertible in $R$ or $R slash p R$ is
  Artinian. If $bold(C)$ is a finite chain complex of finitely generated
  $R G$-modules with $Ext^n_(R G) (bold(C), bold(C)) = 0$ for all $n$
  sufficiently large, then $bold(C)$ has a finite projective resolution.
] <cor:finite-projective-resolution>

#proof[
  If $bold(D)$ is another finite chain complex of finitely generated
  $R G$-modules, then $bold(Hom)_R (bold(C), bold(D))$ is also a finite chain
  complex of finitely generated $R G$-modules, and so by the above theorem
  $Ext^*_(R G) (bold(C), bold(D))$ is Noetherian as a module over the ring
  $Ext^*_(R G) (bold(C), bold(C))$. Since $Ext^n_(R G) (bold(C), bold(C)) = 0$
  for all $n$ sufficiently large, we deduce the same of
  $Ext^n_(R G) (bold(C), bold(D))$.

  Now for each $p$ dividing $|G|$, $(R slash p R)G$ is Artinian, and hence has
  only finitely many simple modules. So we may choose $n_0$ such that for all
  $n > n_0$, all $p$ dividing $|G|$, and all simple $(R slash p R)G$-modules
  $S$, $Ext^n_(R G) (bold(C), S) = 0$. We claim that for all $n > n_0$ and all
  finitely generated $R G$-modules $M$, $Ext^n_(R G) (bold(C), M) = 0$. For
  suppose $Ext^n_(R G) (bold(C), M) != 0$. By Proposition~3.6.17 #source(143)of
  Volume~I, multiplication by $|G|$ annihilates $Ext^n_(R G) (bold(C), M)$. The
  long exact sequences in $Ext$ coming from the short exact sequences of modules
  $ 0 -> abs(G) . M -> M -> M slash abs(G) . M -> 0 $
  and
  $ 0 -> Ker(|G| "on" M) -> M -> abs(G) . M -> 0 $
  give
  $ #multiplication-sequences $
  and so either $Ext^n_(R G) (bold(C), Ker(|G| "on" M))$ or
  $Ext^n_(R G) (bold(C), M slash abs(G) . M)$ is non-zero. Thus we may assume
  that multiplication by $|G|$ on $M$ is zero. Using the long exact sequences
  coming from multiplication by the primes dividing $|G|$ in the same way, we
  may assume that multiplication by $p$ on $M$ is zero for some $p$ dividing
  $|G|$. But now since $(R slash p R)G$ is Artinian, this implies that $M$ has
  finite composition length and so again using the long exact sequences coming
  from a composition series, we see that we may assume that $M$ is simple. But
  we have chosen $n$ so that $Ext^n_(R G) (bold(C), M)$ is zero in this case.

  Now let $bold(P)$ be a projective resolution of $bold(C)$, bounded below, in
  which each $P_n$ is a finitely generated $R G$-module. Choose $n_0$ as above,
  and so that also $C_n = 0$ for $n > n_0$. Then for $n > n_0$,
  $ dots.c -> P_(n+1) -> P_n -> P_(n-1) -> dots.c $
  is exact. Let $M = Im(P_n -> P_(n-1))$. Then
  $
    Hom_(R G) (P_(n-1), M) -> Hom_(R G) (P_n, M) -> Hom_(R G) (P_(n+1), M)
    -> dots.c
  $
  is exact, since $Ext^n_(R G) (bold(C), M) = 0$. The natural homomorphism
  $P_n -> M$ goes to zero in $Hom_(R G) (P_(n+1), M)$, and so it is a composite
  $P_n -> P_(n-1) -> M$. It follows that $M$ is a summand of $P_(n-1)$, so that
  $ 0 -> P_(n-1) slash M -> P_(n-2) -> dots.c -> 0 $
  is a finite projective resolution of $bold(C)$.
]

#exercise[
  #source(144)Let $1 -> Z -> P -> P slash Z -> 1$ be a central extension with
  $P$ a $p$-group and $Z$ cyclic of order $p$, and set $n = |P : Z|$. Let
  $sigma$ be a non-zero element of $H^2 (Z, FF_p) tilde.equiv E_2^(0, 2)$ in the
  spectral sequence
  $ H^* (P slash Z, H^* (Z, FF_p)) => H^* (P, FF_p). $
  Show that $sigma^n$ is a non zero-divisor on every page $E_r^(**)$ of this
  spectral sequence, on which the differential vanishes. Deduce that
  $norm_(Z, P) (sigma)$ is a non zero-divisor in $H^* (P, FF_p)$.

  If $G$ is a finite group, show that the depth#idx("depth") of $H^* (G, FF_p)$
  (see Definition~@def:regular-sequence) is at least the $p$-rank of the centre
  of a Sylow $p$-subgroup of $G$ (Duflot [@bib:Duflot1981])#idx(
    "Duflot's theorem",
  )#idx("theorem", "Duflot's").
]
